#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189371 :tutorial/content "To compute the expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ given a joint mass function $f(x,y),$ we must first find its marginal mass function $f_X.$ Once the marginal mass function is known, we can then compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X]\n= \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n x_i \\, f_X(x_i).\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ |\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Since we're only interested in the expected value of $X,$ let's compute the row totals only.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.20}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.80}$ |\n\nTherefore, the marginal mass function of $X$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f_X(x)$ | $0.20$ | $0.80$ |\n|  |  |  |\n\nWe can now compute the expected value of $X\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\,{f}_{X}({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.2+2\\cdot 0.8 \\\\  & =0.2+1.6 \\\\  & =1.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\textrm{E}[X] = 1.8.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0512a52f-ccb2-56a2-b436-f49ec9cb4916" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6253 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189382 :tutorial/content "To compute the expected value of a *continuous* random variable $X$ given a joint density function $f(x,y),$ we must first find its marginal density function $f_X.$ Once the marginal density function is known, we can then compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X]\n= \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty x \\cdot f_X(x)\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "9ed12512-4300-57a6-b68d-2cacfa2f3488" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6320 :tutorial/title "Continuous Random Variables"}]}
